{"document": "## Boys at play  \nChina, Qing dynasty (1644- 1911), 18th century  Kei (silk tapestry) with metallic threads, panel, 35.8 x 34.4 cm  Gift of Dr Lam Kwok Pun  HKUT2008.1666  \nWoven on a richly embellished golden ground with polychrome threads, the children represent a popular design known as Yingzi tu (Boys at play). Surrounded by a pleasing scene of trees, mountains, flowers and a pagoda, each child strikes a unique expression and pose. During the Ming (1368- 1644) and Qing dynasties, the Yingzi tu design appeared on textiles, furnishings, ceramics and other decorative objects to represent the desire for a large family.  \nIn the mid- Qing dynasty, kei weaving techniques and technologies were sufficiently advanced to allow for the widespread production of textiles with identical double- sided imagery that did not cross the threads on either side- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -  - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -   - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -    - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -     - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -      - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -       - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -        - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -         - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -          - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -           - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -            - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -             - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -              - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -               - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -                - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -                 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -                  - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -                   - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -                    - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -                     - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -                      - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -                       - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -